THINK = {
1: r"Physics starts by describing, not explaining. Pick a direction as positive, name a starting point, and let velocity and acceleration be how position and velocity change. When you derive a formula from these definitions instead of memorising it, you can rebuild it whenever you forget.",
2: r"Newton's second law is a recipe: find every force on one object, add them as vectors, and divide by the mass. The free-body diagram is how you keep the list honest. If you draw it every time, even hard problems become the same routine.",
3: r"Momentum is the quantity that survives collisions, as long as nothing outside pushes. The skill is choosing your system so that the forces you cannot compute are internal. Then you can predict what happens after, without knowing the messy details of the crash.",
4: r"Energy is bookkeeping for motion: it changes form but is never lost, and when it seems to disappear it has gone into heat or sound. Often the shortest route to a speed or height is to compare energy at two moments and skip the forces entirely.",
5: r"Rotation has the same story as straight-line motion, with new names: angle for position, torque for force, moment of inertia for mass. What is new is that where you push matters as much as how hard. Keep the translation table in mind and the chapter feels familiar.",
6: r"Angular momentum is the spinning counterpart of momentum, and it is conserved when no outside twist acts. That is why a skater spins faster when her arms come in. Look for symmetries and conserved quantities before reaching for the force equations.",
7: r"Anything pushed slightly away from a stable position and pulled back behaves like a spring. That is why the maths of the spring shows up everywhere. Damping and driving add friction and a push, and resonance appears when the push matches the natural rhythm.",
8: r"Gravity is a force that gets weaker with distance, and an orbit is what happens when you fall but keep missing. Think in conserved quantities: energy and angular momentum decide the whole shape. Kepler's laws are consequences, not extra facts.",
9: r"The electric field is a way of saying that charges change the space around them. Instead of asking what one charge does to another, ask what field the first creates, then what that field does to the second. Superposition (add the contributions) is the real working law.",
10: r"Gauss's law turns a hard integral into a counting argument when the situation is symmetric: count how many field lines leave a closed surface. The craft is choosing the surface cleverly. If there is no symmetry, the law is still true but not helpful.",
11: r"Potential is energy per charge, a single number at each point, which is easier to work with than a vector field. Nature moves charges downhill in potential, and the field is the slope. Capacitors store energy by holding charge at a potential difference.",
12: r"A circuit is a conservation law with wires: charge is not lost at junctions, and energy is not created around a loop. These two rules solve every network. Time dependence appears when a capacitor charges, and then the answer is an exponential.",
13: r"Magnetism is what electricity looks like when charges move. The magnetic force steers a charge but never speeds it up. When you meet a magnetic problem, ask which charges are moving, and picture the direction with the right-hand rule.",
14: r"Faraday's law says a changing magnetic flux creates an electric push, and Lenz's law says the push opposes the change. Opposition is nature's way of conserving energy. An inductor resists changes in current the way a heavy flywheel resists changes in speed.",
15: r"Maxwell noticed that a changing electric field acts like a current and so makes a magnetic field. With that missing piece, a self-sustaining wave of electric and magnetic fields appears, travelling at the speed of light. Light is electromagnetism.",
16: r"All waves share a few ideas: speed, wavelength, frequency, and the way waves add. Interference is simply adding waves, crest on crest or crest on trough. Most of optics follows from asking how far each path is, measured in wavelengths.",
17: r"Heat is the energy of jostling molecules, and temperature is its average. The first law is energy conservation with heat added. The second says that heat engines always waste some, and Carnot shows how little they must.",
18: r"Machines that convert power are Faraday's law used on purpose. Generators, motors and transformers all rely on a changing flux. Efficiency is about the losses, so always ask where the energy is going.",
19: r"Contest problems are a test of method, not memory. Read, draw, choose the principle (force, energy, momentum, conservation), solve with symbols, and check units and limits. If you are stuck, the hint was written to nudge you, not to give the answer.",
}
